[1m[95m[NARRATION][0m 
  You reach into your pack, fingers brushing past rations and cordage until 
  they close around the familiar weight of the brass compass. It's warm from 
  the morning's humidity, the metal casing slick against your palm as you pull 
  it free. Mara's eyes track the motion. She straightens slightly, her 
  attention sharpening. You hold it out to her. The compass swings gently on 
  its lanyard, catching a glint of copper lamplight. But Mara doesn't take it. 
  Her gaze flicks from the compass to your face, and something shifts in her 
  expression — a flicker of surprise, then calculation, then something almost 
  like reluctance. She lets the moment stretch, the silence between you filling 
  with the hiss of rain against the shutters. "That's not a trinket, Kael." Her 
  voice is lower now. "That compass has been in your family longer than the Ash 
  Gate has been sealed. You sure you want it out of your hands?" She hasn't 
  moved to accept it. Her hands remain flat on the table, one still resting on 
  the sketch of the Gate. "I can keep it safe. I *will* keep it safe. But I 
  need you to be certain. Because once it's in my coat, it's not something you 
  can reach for in a hurry." The tavern creaks around you. Through the 
  floorboards, a draft carries the faint, acrid smell of ash. Mara holds your 
  gaze. "So. Are you sure?" 

