[1m[95m[NARRATION][0m 
  Mara's hand hovers over the table as she finishes pulling her glove tight. 
  She's already half-turned toward the door, the conversation settling into 
  motion — when you reach into your pouch. The coins clink as you set them on 
  the scarred wood. Two gold pieces, catching the copper lamp-light. She stops. 
  Looks down at them. Then back up at you, one eyebrow lifting a fraction. 
  "That's not necessary." But she doesn't push them back. Her fingers tap once 
  against the table's edge, considering. Then she scoops them up — a single, 
  economical motion — and drops them into her coat pocket without counting. 
  "I'll buy the next round of information with it." A thin smile, the first 
  crack in her severity since you sat down. "But know this — I'd have done the 
  work for free. The coin tells me you *value* what we're walking into. Good. 
  You should." She turns toward the door, one hand resting on the latch. The 
  rain has softened to a drizzle, light filtering through the shutters' gaps. 
  "The Gate's a hundred paces east. Through the yard, past the old crane. I'll 
  show you the footprints myself." She cracks the door. A gust of damp air 
  curls in, carrying the smell of wet stone and cold ash. "Coming?" 
