[1m[95m[NARRATION][0m 
  The smoke still curls from the crater. The tavern's silence rings in your 
  ears, punctuated by the drip of rain through a cracked shutter. Mara's eyes 
  haven't left you. That sharp, recalibrating look — she's reevaluating 
  everything you've said since you walked in. Then a sound cuts through the 
  quiet. Footsteps. Heavy boots on wet cobblestones. Coming from the yard side 
  of the tavern. Not casual. Paced. Purposeful. Mara's head snaps toward the 
  door. Her hand drops to her belt. Another sound — a muffled voice outside. 
  Low. A question asked, answered by someone she can't see. Her jaw tightens. 
  She looks at the door, then back at the smoking ruin where the reliquary 
  stood. Then at you. One last look. Hard. Reading. She doesn't speak. She 
  moves. A single fluid motion — she's through the back door before the latch 
  finishes clicking. The door swings shut. Her footsteps don't pause on the 
  other side — they fade, quick and deliberate, heading away from the 
  approaching boots. She's gone. The drizzle continues. The copper lamps 
  flicker. Outside, the footsteps grow closer. The door creaks. You're alone 
  with the smoke and the splintered table and the weight of the obsidian key in 
  your pack. 

