[1m[95m[NARRATION][0m 
  "Old Bram." The name falls into the empty tavern. No echo answers. Just the 
  hiss of rain, the creak of cooling timbers, the soft drip from the cracked 
  shutter. You didn't expect a reply. You didn't want one. But the name pulls 
  something loose — a memory, rising through the smoke and the damp wood smell. 
  You were young. The Ash Gate was just a story then, something older children 
  whispered about after dark. But Old Bram had been real to you. A figure 
  hunched at the end of the south pier, his skiff tied to the same post every 
  evening, his eyes the colour of ash and river silt. You'd asked him once, 
  bold with youth, what lay beyond the Gate. He'd looked at you. Long enough 
  that you thought he wouldn't answer. Then he'd said: *"Nothing that wants to 
  be found."* He'd spat into the water. And that was all. The memory fades. The 
  tavern is quiet. The door at the front groans — a boot testing the threshold. 
  Through the smoke, you see the crater where the reliquary stood. The obsidian 
  key sits heavy in your pack. *Nothing that wants to be found.* But someone 
  crossed three nights ago. And they didn't come back. 

