[1m[95m[NARRATION][0m 
  The back door swings shut behind you. The drizzle has softened to a mist, 
  clinging to your coat and the cobblestones underfoot. The Copper Finch's 
  copper lamps cast orange puddles through the rain, but you don't look back. 
  The yard stretches ahead — packed earth, puddled with standing water, flanked 
  by an old stone wall on one side and a rusted crane on the other. Its arm 
  juts skyward, pulley chain swaying in the damp breeze. You cross the yard. 
  Past the crane. Past a heap of broken masonry overgrown with moss. The ground 
  slopes gently upward, then levels out. And there it is. The Ash Gate. It 
  rises from a bed of disturbed ash, a cracked arch of black stone that seems 
  to drink the morning light rather than reflect it. The arch is perhaps twelve 
  feet tall, its inner surface smooth as polished jet — save for the fracture 
  that runs from the keystone down the left pillar, a jagged seam where 
  something *gave*. Ashes surround its base in a wide circle. Grey. Fine. Like 
  the remains of a fire that burned so hot it left nothing behind. And in those 
  ashes — footprints. Fresh. Leading in. Not out. The air is still here. The 
  drizzle doesn't reach the arch. A faint warmth radiates from the stone, like 
  a kiln that cooled hours ago. The obsidian key in your pack seems heavier 
  than before. 

